\chapter{Historical and bibliographical notes}
\label{chap:h01}
\section{Measures and distributions}
\label{sec:history-measures}
The representation of positive functionals by measures is the Riesz representation theorem. The locally compact, $\sigma$-compact formulation is discussed in \cite[Theorem 24]{Tao09Radon}; \cref{thm:positive-functional-measure} gives a proof on Euclidean open sets by a metric outer measure and level-set approximation. The regularity and density statements in \cref{prop:borel-regularity,prop:continuous-density} correspond to \cite[Theorem 21, Exercise 22 and Proposition 10]{Tao09Radon}. The metric outer-measure argument includes the measurability construction; compare \cite[Theorem 5]{Tao10Products}. The product-measure and integral-interchange assertions in \cref{prop:tonelli-fubini} are the $\sigma$-finite Tonelli--Fubini theorem; compare \cite[Theorem 36 and its proof]{Tao10Products}.

\section{Harmonic functions and boundary values}
\label{sec:history-harmonic}
The fundamental solutions, distributional regularity, Poisson formula and Harnack inequality appear in \cite[Theorems 3.1.2--3.1.7, pp.~117--123]{Hor07}. That source's fundamental solution has $\Delta E=\delta_0$, as in \cref{prop:laplacian-fundamental-solution}; our positive Green kernel instead solves $-\Delta G=\delta_x$. \Cref{thm:weyl} proves the homogeneous distributional regularity statement directly by radial averaging and convolution, without the analytic continuation assertion of that source's Theorem 3.1.3. \Cref{thm:ball-dirichlet,prop:harnack-ball} retain the explicit Poisson kernel and its two-sided bounds. Dimension one is included separately in these statements.

\section{Subharmonic functions and polar sets}
\label{sec:history-subharmonic}
The comparison and mean characterizations, local integrability, potentials and distributional characterization are developed in \cite[Theorem 3.2.3, Corollary 3.2.8 and Theorems 3.2.9--3.2.11, pp.~143--148]{Hor07}. \Cref{thm:subharmonic-comparison,prop:subharmonic-local-integrability,thm:riesz-decomposition} give these interfaces before \cref{thm:subharmonic-distribution} constructs the canonical distributional representative. The sphere means prove uniqueness pointwise. Our Riesz measure is $\Delta u$, with no factor $1/(2\pi)$, so a plane logarithmic pole has mass $2\pi$.

\section{Energy, capacity and exceptional sets}
\label{sec:history-capacity}
The continuity principle and the construction of continuous-potential restrictions in \cref{thm:potential-continuity-principle} follow the argument of \cite[Theorems 3.4.6--3.4.7, pp.~207--208]{Hor07}; \cref{lem:lusin-finite-measure} supplies the measure-theoretic approximation used there. The compact polarity criterion is \cite[Theorem 3.4.5, pp.~206--208]{Hor07}. Our relative capacity is the total Riesz mass of the regularized extremal function, with $\Delta u$ as measure and the positive kernel solving $-\Delta G=\delta$. It should therefore not be identified numerically with a differently normalized capacity.

\section{Fine topology and balayage}
\label{sec:history-fine}
Fuglede attributes the classical fine topology to Cartan and the quasi-Lindel\"of principle to Doob in \cite[Introduction, p.~227]{Fug71Fine}. His preliminaries \cite[Section 1, pp.~228--230]{Fug71Fine} work in an axiomatic harmonic space with a domination axiom and a positive potential; they assume general sweeping duality before studying fine connectedness. Our \cref{thm:fine-quasi-lindelof} instead follows from the Euclidean polar envelope discrepancy and Choquet selection already proved. \Cref{ex:uncountable-polar-cantor} supplies the countability obstruction explicitly.

\section{Fourier analysis and complex-line incidence}
\label{sec:history-fourier-incidence}
For the Euclidean Schwartz-space inversion and Plancherel argument compare \cite[Exercises 38--42, formula (11), Theorem 43 and Exercise 44]{Tao09Fourier}. \Cref{lem:fourier-gaussian,thm:plancherel} use a real differential equation for the Gaussian transform, Gaussian damping for inversion, and simultaneous $L^1$ and $L^2$ approximation for compatibility of the two transforms. The convention is $\mathrm e^{-2\pi\mathrm i x\cdot\xi}$. Gaussian integration gives the exact Riesz-energy constant in \cref{thm:riesz-fourier-energy}; the kernel there is $|x-y|^{-s}$ without the fundamental-solution normalization.

\section{Differential forms, currents and holomorphic divisors}
\label{sec:history-complex-currents}
The positivity cones, their restriction criterion and the order-zero estimate can be compared with \cite[Chapter III, Section 1.A--B, pp.~129--134, especially (1.4), (1.6) and (1.14)]{DemBook}. \Cref{prop:positivity-cones} also proves the interior property needed for trace bounds by a finite average over coordinate signs and permutations. Our currents use bidegree; Demailly's current subscripts there use bidimension. The maps in \cref{prop:proper-current-pushforward,prop:submersion-current-pullback} correspond to \cite[Chapter III, (1.17), p.~134]{DemBook}, with properness required on the current's support.

\section{Plurisubharmonic functions and invariant minima}
\label{sec:history-plurisubharmonic}
The automatic upper semicontinuity theorem \cref{thm:automatic-psh-usc} completes the argument of the supplied unsigned manuscript \cite[Theorem 1.1 and complete proof, pp.~1--6]{PSHD}. Its Lemma 1.2, pp.~1--2, proves measurability by induction using bounded fiber means, and Proposition 2.1, pp.~4--5, identifies the given finite line-subharmonic function with a canonical representative. Its final proof, p.~6, uses truncation. The incidence prerequisite was reconstructed in \cref{chap:p06}; \cref{prop:positive-hessian-holomorphic-composition} supplies the canonical representative's line restrictions independently of the upper semicontinuity theorem. Thus the usual definition remains in \cref{def:plurisubharmonic}, with its redundant condition proved immediately afterwards.

\section{Bounded products and nonlinear capacity}
\label{sec:history-bounded-products}
The recursive construction, bounded CLN estimates and decreasing continuity are developed in \cite[Chapter III, Section 3, (3.1)--(3.11), pp.~144--150]{DemBook}. \Cref{thm:cln-bounded} gives the bounded Chern--Levine--Nirenberg estimate. \Cref{lem:bt-common-collar,thm:bt-decreasing-continuity} supply the compact-difference integration-by-parts identity and the weighted induction, obtaining symmetry at each stage before passing its weighted limit. The weighted $L^1$ estimate \cref{thm:cln-weighted} has base current $1$ and keeps the collar term; it does not extend that estimate to an arbitrary singular current.

\section{Pluripolar sets and the plurifine topology}
\label{sec:history-pluripolar}
Josefson proved the equivalence of local and global pluripolarity on $\mathbb C^n$ in \cite{Jos78}. The capacity approach is due to Bedford--Taylor \cite{BT82}; compare the relative-extremal construction in \cite[Theorem 4.32, pp.~118--119, Theorem 4.34, pp.~119--121, and Theorem 4.40 and Corollary 4.41, pp.~124--125]{GZ17}. \Cref{prop:relative-extremal-compact,prop:relative-extremal-open} prove the compact and open mass identities after homogeneous balayage. \Cref{thm:zero-capacity-pluripolar} needs only those identities and decreasing open covers; \cref{thm:josefson} checks the logarithmic collars and the local integrability of the global sum explicitly.

\Needspace{19\baselineskip}
\section{Weighted estimates and local extension}
\label{sec:history-weighted-extension}
The Hilbert adjoint and operator-complex arguments can be compared with \cite[Chapter VIII, Section 1, pp.~363--365]{DemBook}. \Cref{prop:hilbert-projection-representation,prop:hilbert-weak-sequences} prove the Hilbert foundations used here. The interior three-weight construction is presented in \cite[Lemma 7.2, Proposition 7.3 and Theorem 7.1, pp.~35--39]{Blo14SCV}. \Cref{lem:three-weight-density} checks cutoff multiplication in the Hilbert adjoint domain before smoothing, giving \cref{thm:hormander-smooth-weight}. \Cref{thm:hormander-singular-lower-bound} treats a prescribed smooth semipositive Hessian lower bound by approximations whose right sides are pointwise bounded. For \cref{lem:richberg-euclidean,thm:oka-boundary-distance} compare \cite[Chapter I, (5.18)--(5.21), pp.~43--44, and (7.1)--(7.2), pp.~53--55]{DemBook}. These give the invariant exhaustion needed by \cref{cor:kiselman-pseudoconvex-tube}.

\section{Lelong numbers and multiplier ideals}
\label{sec:history-lelong-multiplier}
The Jensen--Lelong formula can be compared with \cite[Chapter III, (6.1)--(6.11), pp.~162--166]{DemBook}. \Cref{thm:euclidean-jensen-lelong} proves its Euclidean ball form directly from Stokes' formula and spherical means. Demailly's operator there is twice ours, and his growth variable is $\log|z|$; the density in \eqref{eq:lelong-ball-density} uses our $\ddc$ and $\log|z|^2$, giving $\nu(\log|z|^2,0)=1$.

\section{Analytic singularities and regularization}
\label{sec:history-analytic-regularization}
The local Bergman approximation is Demailly's construction; compare \cite[Proposition 3.1 and its proof, pp.~13--14]{DemRegOnline} and \cite[Theorem 14.2, pp.~136--138]{DemAnalyticOnline}. Those sources use the weight $\mathrm e^{-2m\varphi}$ and a prefactor $(2m)^{-1}$; \cref{thm:local-bergman-approximation} uses $\mathrm e^{-m\varphi}$ and $m^{-1}$, with our Lelong normalization. The finite-generator comparison and resolved smoothness of \cref{lem:bergman-smoothness-and-resolution} are made explicit using the earlier bounded Hilbert-generation proof.

\Needspace{11\baselineskip}
\section{Global potentials and positivity loci}
\label{sec:history-global-potentials}
For the elliptic framework and the real form Laplacian compare \cite[Chapter VI, Section 2, pp.~289--290, and Sections 3.1--3.2, pp.~291--294]{DemBook}. \Cref{lem:compact-elliptic-foundations} proves the needed Sobolev, compactness and regularity ingredients directly by Fourier truncation, difference quotients and duality. \Cref{thm:global-ddc-lemma} uses the scalar trace equation and the primitive $(1,1)$ star identity; its factor $-1/(4\pi)$ reflects our operator and the nonnegative Riemannian Laplacian.

\section{Convex duality and real Monge--Amp\`ere measures}
\label{sec:history-convex-measures}
For \cref{lem:finite-dimensional-convex-separation,prop:convex-local-regularity-and-subgradients} compare \cite[Theorem 2.1.10 and Corollaries 2.1.11--2.1.13, pp.~44--45; Theorem 2.1.22, pp.~55--56]{Hor07}. The closed-epigraph and conjugation arguments are in \cite[Propositions 2.2.1--2.2.2 and Theorem 2.2.4, pp.~66--68; Theorem 2.2.5, pp.~68--69; Theorem 2.2.8, p.~70]{Hor07}. These complete arguments were checked in the retained 2007 reprint. \Cref{thm:finite-dimensional-fenchel-duality,prop:convex-envelope-algebra} supply the internal duality interfaces; in the attained sum formula both primal functions are finite on the whole space.
